00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.760
So, before we dive
in, just a show

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.620
of hands, how many of
you in the audience

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.980
are getting ready to do a
large scale migration to GCP?

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.040
Wow, pretty good.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.520
How about those of you that are
doing cloud to cloud migration,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.430
as well as on prem in the cloud?

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:21.990
Awesome.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.420
Well, the reason I
bring that up first

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.620
is, I've been in professional
services for hyperscale cloud

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.930
organizations now for
almost five years.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.410
And the conversations that
I am having today compared

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.390
to what I was having
five years ago

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.540
are an order of magnitude
different from the perspective

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.070
of where public cloud
is as a priority for IT.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.380
4 and 1/2 years ago,
the public cloud,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.540
it was all about
experimentation,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.510
getting your feet wet,
trying to understand how

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.010
to leverage public cloud.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.680
Fast forward to today,
it's all about mission

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.290
critical workloads,
moving entire data

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.210
centers to the cloud, and
how we can do it quickly,

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.980
faster and efficiently.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.000
And that's where
Velostrata comes into play.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.190
I also want to take a
look, beyond my firsthand

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.740
observations, at this survey.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.440
This is a recent survey
of IT cloud professionals.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.440
And they were asked, what
are your top IT cloud

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.930
initiatives for 2019?

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.885
The first one, not
surprisingly, is the things

00:02:20.885 --> 00:02:22.260
that I got into
the public cloud.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.480
I want to optimize their cost
and save my organization money.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.750
But the other two are very
relevant to today's session.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.750
The second being, I want
to move more workloads.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.530
Right?

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.970
And the third is, I want to
more quickly adopt containers.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.430
Containers are the new VM.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.490
And everybody is
aware of how exciting

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.115
they are and all of the benefits
that you get from running GKE.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.567
But let's imagine
that, for a second,

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.650
you wanted to take your
first ever trip to Europe.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.822
Would you just jump on
the airplane and go?

00:02:51.822 --> 00:02:52.530
I don't think so.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.390
You would do some research.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.340
You would buy a travel guide.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.290
Your cloud migration
journey is no different.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.050
You want to learn
from professionals

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.630
who have been there, who have
done that, who have seen it.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.850
And that's where Google
Cloud professional services,

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.900
and all of Google, is here to
help you with that journey.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.390
Especially when you see a
metric like on this slide, where

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.510
94% of respondents
to this survey

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.370
said that they face
some sort of cost

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.520
overrun, or some sort
of timing challenge,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.710
with their migration journey.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.890
Some of the aspects that we like
to highlight when considering

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.220
your own migration journey
are like at the top

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.330
here, where it says scale.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.340
So are you guys
going to approach it

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.370
from just a few VM's,
a few workloads,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.080
or are you considering
an entire data

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.190
center moved to public cloud?

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.080
Multi source.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.780
So where the
workloads coming from?

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.160
Are you looking at bare metal?

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.752
Are you looking at virtualized
in your data center?

00:03:46.752 --> 00:03:48.210
Or are you looking
at other clouds,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.280
potentially, that,
for whatever reason,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.525
you want to move that to GCP.

00:03:52.525 --> 00:03:54.150
The next attribute
you want to consider

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.040
is complexity of the workload.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.230
So is it a multi-tier app?

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.965
Is it a monolith?

00:03:59.965 --> 00:04:02.340
What is it that you're wanting
to move up into the cloud?

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.233
And how does that impact
your migration timelines

00:04:05.233 --> 00:04:06.900
and expertise that
you need at the table

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.680
to do it successfully?

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.980
And then, finally, risk.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.960
So, most people, when they
want to move VMs to the cloud,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:14.950
they do it in waves.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.730
So let's imagine on a Friday
night during a change window,

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.709
you wanted to move some
VMs, and one of them

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.110
doesn't move correctly
and gets hung up.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.870
Do you have a mechanism
to roll that back and not

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.200
impact your application owners
and not impact your business?

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.120
Because you want to
do your migration,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.830
but you also don't want to
do it in a disruptive manner.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:33.810
And then the one
thing that's not

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.185
on this slide I
want to highlight

00:04:35.185 --> 00:04:38.070
is, don't forget about the
people and process aspects

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:38.940
as well.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.760
So involve your
application owners.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.250
Make sure that the
first time they

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.030
hear about your VM migrations
is not an email the week before.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.370
You want to involve them
early so that they understand

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.290
the value proposition,
why you're

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.390
doing it to make their
applications better,

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.550
their application
development processes better,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.750
and their businesses more
reliable in performance.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.810
You guys have probably seen
this slide a lot this week.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.860
And this is our
methodology for migrations.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.650
And the reason we use this
a lot is because it works.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.960
We have driven improvement.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.880
And this is from
being in the field

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.070
and learning and seeing
multiple organizations

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.150
approach public cloud.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.010
And it's the best
way to approach it.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:23.380
So if one of you called
me tomorrow and said,

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.310
Joe, I would love your
professional services team

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.070
to help us move out of our
data center next month,

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.348
I would start with
this exact methodology.

00:05:31.348 --> 00:05:32.640
I would start with a discovery.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:33.348
What do you have?

00:05:33.348 --> 00:05:34.980
What are the workloads?

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.050
What do your
applications look like?

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:40.810
How interdependent are they
on the other applications?

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.830
Next, we would look at
your GCP foundations.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.890
You have to build a really
solid foundation for the success

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.890
of your overall GCP initiative.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.230
And, from experience,
I will tell you

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.270
that the primary
areas that you really

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.830
want to focus on
around foundations

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.410
are security and networking.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.160
You have to get your
ingress, your egress,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.480
figured out correctly.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.975
And you obviously have to
include your security team

00:06:02.975 --> 00:06:04.350
to make sure that
you adopt cloud

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:07.740
in a way that conforms with
your organization's security

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.200
controls.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.800
So you've got your
applications figured out,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.320
you've got your landing zone
and your foundation built,

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.240
now you need to start
migrating workloads.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.550
And that's, really,
what today's session

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.530
is all about, the tools
that we have to offer

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.180
you to accelerate that.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.090
And I'm going to hold that
thought to my next slide.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.470
And then, finally, optimization.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.677
So once you get the workloads
there into the public cloud,

00:06:32.677 --> 00:06:34.260
what can you do to
make things better?

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.780
And this isn't always
just about the app.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.750
Here at Google, we invented
sight reliability engineering.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.390
So maybe you want to
take your operations team

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.060
and make them think more of in
cloud manner versus an on prem

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:45.750
manner.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.780
Maybe you want to
reinvigorate your CI/CD,

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.720
or DevOps processes,
or even start

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.430
to refactor your applications
into microservices.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.190
And, OK, let's double
click into migration.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.230
So what are some of
the methods that you

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:05.460
can adopt when considering
how you're going to migrate?

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.460
So the two most
common that we see

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.630
are re-host re-platform,
where we see around 60 to 70%

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:15.180
of organizations
approaching it this way.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.720
And this is traditional
lift and shift.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.300
And this is typically
the chosen method

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.380
because it's all about speed.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:26.250
It's all about, I want to
get my workloads to the cloud

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.860
as quickly as I can and
efficiently as I can.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.200
And this is the sweet
spot of Velostrata

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.400
and why so many organizations
use it in their GCP migrations.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.660
The second is all about
lift and modernize.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.930
So earlier this week, we
introduced the next iteration

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.450
of Velostrata, Anthos Migrate.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:49.440
And to talk more about that tech
and how Anthos Migrate works

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.420
I want to invite my
friend Issy to the stage.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:52.196
Issy?

00:07:52.196 --> 00:07:55.168
[AUDIENCE APPLAUDS]

00:07:55.168 --> 00:07:55.960
ISSY BEN-SHAUL: OK.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:56.460
Thank you.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:01.825
Hello everyone.

00:08:01.825 --> 00:08:03.450
Thanks for coming to
the session today.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.430
Thanks, Joe.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:11.670
So I will talk, in
my session, about how

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:16.680
Anthos Migrate actually performs
the migration and what it does.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:20.490
And we'll leave enough
time for my partner, Lewis,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:23.280
to talk about how they actually
use the system in order

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.220
to migrate thousands of VM's.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.530
And we'll focus, here, on the--

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.540
Anthros Migrate, just
for terminology's sake,

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.679
is actually the broad name
for all replacing Velostrata.

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:36.760
I might be confusing myself
now when I'm talking.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:38.980
I'm still used to Velostrata.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:42.240
But Anthos Migrate refers
to both the ability

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.010
to move VM's in
a lift and shift,

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.320
as well as the new capability
that was introduced today,

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.010
which I will talk about
more towards the end

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:53.700
of the session in the road map.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.350
So how does it actually work?

00:08:56.350 --> 00:09:00.270
So, essentially, the way that
Anthros Migrate is deployed

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.730
is, you take, basically, a
virtual appliance that you

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:04.860
deploy on premises.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:08.238
And from then on, there is
another set of appliances.

00:09:08.238 --> 00:09:10.030
This is what you see
on the left hand side.

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:13.500
And on the right hand
side, we automatically

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.630
deployed, in a
scalable fashion, a set

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:20.760
of two or more
instances that basically

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.710
are termed cloud extension.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.920
But they're actually
used as sort of a proxy

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.540
to the on prem environment.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.380
And, essentially, what
we do is, once you set up

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.560
a VPN and a VPC that
is set up, you're

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:35.960
ready to go and
migrate to scale.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.430
So as Joe said, there is
a discovery session that

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.440
needs to take place early on.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.810
And, basically, it ends
up with identifying

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:48.030
a set of VM's of workloads
that need to migrate as a set

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:52.080
into the public cloud or GCP.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:56.910
And this set of workloads can be
either identified interactively

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.780
through the vCenter
plugin that we provide,

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.360
or through what we
call a runbook, which

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.587
is a utility that does
it in a batch form,

00:10:05.587 --> 00:10:07.170
or even through your
own orchestration

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.120
tool of your choice
could be unstable

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.360
or any other orchestration
tool that you

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.690
have that can be actually
using our APIs to orchestrate

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:16.497
the solution.

00:10:16.497 --> 00:10:18.330
The bottom line, you
have a set of workloads

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.080
that you want to run,
and move, and scale.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.280
So all you do is basically hit
what was called running cloud,

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.520
and when that happens,
essentially, all the VMs

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:30.090
are shut down
gracefully on-premises

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.940
using our on-premises
[INAUDIBLE] appliances.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:35.760
And then they're actually
streamed consistently

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.170
into the public cloud.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.970
And we do it in a way that in
a matter of 5 to 10 minutes,

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.430
those workloads are up
and running in the cloud.

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.200
Now how could that
be if they have

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.713
a lot of databases and stateful
data associated with that?

00:10:48.713 --> 00:10:50.130
That's the question
that comes up.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:51.990
So, in the native
implementation,

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:53.170
that would not work.

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.990
So what we do here
is, actually, we

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.990
implemented one
optimized transport layer

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.640
that works in the way that even
though the compute is running

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.760
in the cloud and the
storage is still remote,

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.120
the performance is not degraded.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.450
We use things like
predictive prefetch

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:12.750
of workloads that need to come
a priori before the users are

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:16.750
using them, caching, of course,
deduplication, compression,

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:18.750
things that we've been
working on for many years

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:23.370
to actually optimize and reduce
the impact of the latency.

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.250
So the bottom line is we have--

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.728
A set of VMs are running,
boot up natively in the cloud

00:11:28.728 --> 00:11:30.270
while the storage
is remote, and when

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.070
you access the
storage or the data,

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.220
you access through the caching--

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:36.690
The appliances are in
the cloud, and they

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.030
fetch the data optimally
from their remote storage.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.510
So this basically
brings you a set of VM

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:45.700
that operate in the
cloud in minutes, right?

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:48.288
Downtime for the production
applications is minimal,

00:11:48.288 --> 00:11:49.830
could be five 10
minutes, and they're

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:54.150
up and running, which is
very important for validation

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:55.348
and for downtime purposes.

00:11:55.348 --> 00:11:56.390
It can be up and running.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:11:59.700
Now, once this is happening
in the background,

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.320
we kick off a
background process that

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.335
actually starts to
migrate the call

00:12:03.335 --> 00:12:05.460
data, the data that they're
just not being accessed

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.200
right now by the applications.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.270
And it runs seamlessly
without disrupting

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:12.000
the operation of the overall
workload that are already

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:12.910
running.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:14.930
Once that data completes
this migration,

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.680
we're ready to go to
the next stage, which

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.780
is the conversion into
completely the cloud storage.

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:22.900
But for everything,
take the cache out,

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.910
flushing it, and getting
out of the data path,

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.700
and then run natively.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.350
It's interesting,
though, to know

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.810
that we have many
customers that are staying

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.760
in this stage for
a while, because

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.350
an interesting
capability you offer

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.450
is the ability to
actually write back

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.780
the data that is being generated
in the cloud back to on-prem.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.050
Why do we do that?

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:44.130
Because we want to allow
customers to roll back

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:46.710
if they need it because,
for whatever reasons,

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.930
the workload didn't
work as expected.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.030
The performance isolates.

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.720
Some misconfiguration,
accessing too much,

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.910
a component is
on-prem, and latency

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.320
is a problem, et cetera.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:02.140
And we've run into that, but the
ability to roll back instantly

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:04.520
gives you the option
to move faster.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:05.772
I know that some customers--

00:13:05.772 --> 00:13:07.480
I think Cardinal as
well-- had like maybe

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:11.230
5% of the workloads rolled back,
fixed, and then rolled forward.

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.280
So it's a very
interesting capability.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:16.630
However, when you're ready
to cut the cord, so to speak,

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.940
you can actually do the
detached convert to completely

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.970
native storage, and off you go.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:26.620
So this of at a high level, the
way that the system operates.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.050
And Louis will
talk more about how

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:33.670
this is being operationalized
into a whole process

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:35.630
in which you decide the
experience, et cetera.

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:38.530
I will let Louis
elaborate on that.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.870
One thing that's really
interesting about this

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:42.940
solution-- and it's
important for you--

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.200
is that is it an
ageless solution.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.470
So one thing we don't do,
we don't install agents

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:49.150
on the workloads, right?

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.740
It's all done sort
of under the covers

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:53.740
through the hypervisor
access to the storage.

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.270
And what that means,
you don't have

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:57.760
to worry about opening firewall
rules for those application

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:00.910
workloads, you don't have
to worry about compatibility

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:04.720
with the antivirus and
interference with workloads.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.810
As you know, I'm
sure enterprise guys

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.090
know that, if you can
avoid agents, it's better.

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:14.050
It also makes the whole
cutover process much more easy

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:14.890
and seamless.

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:17.648
So that's the solution
in a nutshell.

00:14:17.648 --> 00:14:18.565
So let's move forward.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:22.960
One of the things
that we invested in

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:26.860
is making it really easy
for enterprise customers

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.090
to start off.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:30.250
And for that, we
integrated it very nicely

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:32.890
with the VMware vSphere.

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:35.680
We have a vCenter plugin
that plugs right into that

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:38.410
and give you the
experience that you need.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:39.970
If you are a VM
wire operator, you

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.310
don't have to know much
even about the cloud

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:44.440
in order to start
rolling those migrations.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.620
You get monitoring, logging,
built extended here.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:50.860
And everything is done
through the vCenter

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.830
console, both for operation as
well as for monitoring logging.

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:56.680
As I said, if you don't want
to do it, you don't have to.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:00.580
And you can still run from a
web UI or from the runbook,

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:03.250
but that's a very convenient
option to start with.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.780
We also have a very
interesting tool

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:09.520
we call rightsizing
recommendation.

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:12.580
What we found out is there
are a lot of customers that

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:14.440
are worried about cost, right?

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:18.937
If I move like-for-like, my VMs
or other provisioning on-prem--

00:15:18.937 --> 00:15:21.520
Usually, because I don't have
to pay for that other provision.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:22.780
I pay for the host.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:24.550
When I moved to the
cloud, I'm going

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:27.620
to be penalized because I am
going to pay for the instance,

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.245
and I need to pay
for the peak capacity

00:15:29.245 --> 00:15:33.670
in order to make sure that
performance is as needed.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:34.550
That's a problem.

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:39.310
But really what happens is that
with the tool that we offer,

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.380
you can actually
inspect the actual use

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:47.170
of those VMs, what are the
CPU and RAM, usability,

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:48.538
and utility, and utilization.

00:15:48.538 --> 00:15:50.080
And then, with that,
you can actually

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:51.300
come up with recommendations.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:54.070
So you see here, we recommend
if your cost-optimized,

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:57.100
you can actually
cut the cost by 5x,

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.200
compared to what you would
have done if you just

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:03.320
moved like-for-like in terms
of the size of the instance.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:04.900
So it's another
tool in our arsenal

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:10.160
to help you make the
migration more cost-effective,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.360
which is something that your
customers always worry about.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:16.100
Some additional features
are of interest.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:18.510
One thing that we
really care about really

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:20.980
is make sure, again, that
the risk off the migration

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:22.460
is reduced.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:25.570
So we came up with
this utility called

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.510
Tesla, which means you can
test before you migrate.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:29.560
What does it mean?

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:33.550
I can actually take a snapshot
of your VMware environment

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:36.790
and use that consistent
snapshot as a way

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:39.440
to stream it to the cloud
and see if it works.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:43.120
So you don't disrupt that
all the running applications.

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:45.170
All is read-only
copy of the data.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:47.140
And you can write it
locally in the cloud,

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:50.620
and you'll see things
work as they should work.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:52.090
If they don't, nothing happened.

00:16:52.090 --> 00:16:53.650
You didn't disrupt
the application.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.920
So that is a very
powerful tool that

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:00.280
is being used very
extensively by our customers.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.010
We are also in
automation runbook.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.860
I'll let probably Louis
talk more about it.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:07.690
But it's basically a way
to do migration at scale.

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:10.329
As you'll see by Louis'
presentation, when you operate

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:14.260
it properly, you can get
between 50 and 100 VMs

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:17.079
that are actually migrating
at once, and typically,

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:17.710
within a week.

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:22.400
So it could be very effective
with high throughput.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:25.210
We support things like site
level bandwidth throttling,

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.060
because sometimes customers
obviously want to--

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:31.120
They don't have necessary
access to the QoS,

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:33.730
and they need to
control that to secure.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:37.480
And we have also provide
telemetry, very extensive set

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:41.110
of counters for both
monitoring and logging which

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:45.250
is, by the way, now integrated
into Google Stackdriver, which

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:49.450
gives you visibility on
performance, availability,

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:51.280
and so on.

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:52.900
In fact, this is
actually a snapshot

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:55.130
of our telemetry information.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.130
And I wanted to go
and show you what

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:59.900
does it look like in
real-world scenario.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:02.080
Taken from one of our
customers, assuming

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.200
they went by the methodology
that Joe referred to,

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.690
you do discovery,
planning, scheduling.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:10.540
And in the end of
that period, you

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:12.370
come up with a set
of workloads that

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:15.070
need to migrate as a
whole, as a cluster.

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:17.530
Once you do that and
you're ready to kick off

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:19.248
the migration--

00:18:19.248 --> 00:18:20.290
This is using Velostrata.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.750
You can see that, within
literally two hours,

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:27.910
we have about close to 80
VMs up running and GCP.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.830
This is typically
what we see now.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:33.670
The green line is
the actual data

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:35.300
that comes from the background.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:37.360
But it's important to
understand-- realize--

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:41.400
that, from the beginning,
those are operational.

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:42.185
They are running.

00:18:42.185 --> 00:18:43.060
They are operational.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:45.370
And then the data count may
be a day or two days later

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:48.130
until everything completes
the migration in which case

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:51.370
you can detach when
you're ready to move on.

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:53.620
But, if you do it in
a pipeline fashion,

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:58.570
you can get into a very, very
nice cadence of moving again.

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:01.550
80-100 machines within one week.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:04.650
And this is how, for
instance, calling on--

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:06.820
Other customers are able
to move thousands of VMs

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:11.320
within a relatively short period
of time within less than a year

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:13.940
using this methodology.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:19.360
So just to wrap up,
basically, if we

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:23.640
see what are the benefits of
using the Anthos-Migrate for VM

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.990
migration, it's following.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:28.300
The fact that we
can stream workloads

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:32.020
means that you can get quick
validation and reduce downtime.

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:34.870
And within minutes,
you actually get

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:36.880
your systems operated involved.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:40.480
By the way, this is web
vSphere, WebLogic, even SAP.

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:43.960
So all the complex multitude
workloads that are out there

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:48.040
are supported and validated
with a lot of customers

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:49.730
that have used it in the past.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:52.430
So that's one benefit.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:54.850
And then I mentioned it's
agent of solution, which

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:57.160
means that the overhead,
the labor, they

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.890
need to invest in order to
get that solution working.

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:01.450
It's dramatically lower.

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:02.420
It's much seamless.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.210
It's very automated.

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:07.630
All the transition from
moving into on-prem,

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:11.260
into running in the cloud
is completely automated,

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:15.160
injecting drivers that know how
to work with the cloud storage,

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.290
connecting the networks,
reducing and removing

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:19.210
some agents, et cetera,
cleaning up the image.

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:21.590
All this is done automatically.

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:24.800
And that means that
by certain measures,

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:27.340
we compare to traditional
replication solutions

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:28.540
with agents.

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:31.840
It could be easily
five or two 10x less

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.450
overhead in terms of that.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:37.240
I mentioned risk mitigation
critically important

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:41.170
for enterprise CIOs, they
need to make sure the uptime

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:42.700
of their applications.

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:45.200
And that's also very important.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.800
The tools to test
before you migrate,

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.890
which means that you can test
everything before you actually

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.620
decide to move your production
workloads or roll back

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:56.690
if things don't go well
in a matter of minutes.

00:20:56.690 --> 00:20:58.250
So this is also very important.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.360
And last but not least,
the whole completion

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:06.550
of the migration is about 5 to
6x compared to other solutions

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:08.740
faster.

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:13.273
Once you start on a journey
of data center migration, you

00:21:13.273 --> 00:21:15.190
on a boat, you're going
to get it over with as

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:17.260
soon as possible,
because maintaining

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:19.780
two infrastructures,
paying for both.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:23.680
Dealing with synchronization
impacts is a real hassle.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:26.500
You'll be asked
what have we done?

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:29.620
So the sooner you can get
over with the faster--

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:34.120
And this is achieved
through all the things

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:36.340
that we talked about.

00:21:36.340 --> 00:21:40.200
It's just a data point
here of a customer for us

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:43.910
and move 700 VMs,
again, in six month.

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:50.720
And we'll hear more about from
Louis about how they did it.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:55.710
So I think just
to wrap up, we've

00:21:55.710 --> 00:22:00.030
developed those best practices,
determining the workloads

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:01.200
for migration.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.420
Using discovery assessment
is really important.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.640
I think Louis will
reiterate it over that.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:12.670
Building that pipeline, automate
it, and really get going.

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:15.330
So with that, I want
to invite Louis,

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:18.480
and he'll tell you actually they
tools to do it in large scale.

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:19.620
Come on, Louis.

00:22:23.412 --> 00:22:24.620
LOUIS VERZI: Thank you, Issy.

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:30.943
So I want to start
off by telling you

00:22:30.943 --> 00:22:32.360
a little bit about
who is Cardinal

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:36.267
Health, because a lot of you may
not recognize our company name.

00:22:36.267 --> 00:22:37.100
We're not out there.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:39.950
We don't do a lot
of marketing, but we

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:44.640
have over 50,000 employees
throughout the world.

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:48.470
We're a health care
supplier, for the most part.

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:50.770
Supply chain is the big
part of our industry,

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:52.520
but we're moving a lot
into data analytics

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:56.270
and providing other solutions
to the health care market.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:00.200
But we're really about making
health care more efficient,

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:02.810
providers more effective
and, most important, patients

00:23:02.810 --> 00:23:04.730
as healthy as possible.

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:07.940
We're number 14 on the
Fortune 500, 136 billion

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:09.840
in annual revenue.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.050
Our two primary
business segments

00:23:12.050 --> 00:23:15.740
are pharmaceutical and medical.

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:19.820
So what was our main goal
for going to the cloud?

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:22.310
And really, I'm sure
everyone in this room

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:27.170
can appreciate that health care
costs are a primary concern

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:28.820
in many people's minds.

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:31.200
so keeping health
care costs as low

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.560
as humanly possible for
the patients we support,

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:38.540
cost is a huge factor
in our line of business.

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:42.860
So in addition to cost,
the ability to move quickly

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:46.550
and to be agile and to be able
to respond to business needs.

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:48.970
We're huge drivers in our
decision to move to the cloud.

00:23:51.565 --> 00:23:52.690
How can we accomplish that?

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:55.720
How can we accomplish
those cost savings?

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:58.660
We really were out to eliminate
the inefficiencies that

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:01.810
come with managing
on-prem infrastructure,

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:03.850
all the costs that
are both visible

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:06.280
and that aren't
quite so visible.

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:08.705
We looked at how
many hours we spent

00:24:08.705 --> 00:24:10.330
not just from our
infrastructure teams,

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:12.700
but our application
teams on firmware

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:18.040
upgrades, patching, updating
hardware, doing tech refresh.

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:22.570
It was millions of
dollars that the company

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:26.082
was having to spend to keep all
this infrastructure up to date

00:24:26.082 --> 00:24:27.040
and to keep it running.

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:34.210
Just some inefficiency
examples from an on-prem type

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:35.530
environment.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:39.070
Hardware acquisition,
it could take--

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:41.710
I'm sure you all have
procurement processes

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:47.110
that you have a budget,
you have to apply

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:50.410
for funding six months in
advance to your budget year.

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:56.150
And you hope that the $20
million that is available.

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.810
And $80 million worth of
funding were requested.

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:01.660
Are you going to be able to get
any of that funding available?

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:06.190
Those kinds of things,
and months to get hardware

00:25:06.190 --> 00:25:09.220
procured to get it deployed.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:12.370
And everyone over purchased
because they didn't

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:13.720
know how much they needed.

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:17.500
They had to request that funding
months or a year in advance.

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:19.690
By the time they actually
delivered a solution,

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:23.600
there was no money left
available to build upon that.

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:26.200
So they'd just get as
much as they possibly

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:29.830
could to make sure that
they had plenty of room.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:32.740
Application teams are
slowed down significantly

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.470
by unneeded
cross-functional needs.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:37.510
We are a very
siloed organization.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:39.340
If you needed a
system built, there

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:40.840
was seven different
teams you needed

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:44.390
to engage to get that virtual
machine up and running.

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:46.970
And there's a lot of time
that's lost in that too.

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:49.030
So it's not just getting
the amount of labor

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.520
that spent there but
the amount of churn time

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:56.380
that you get to actually
spend those systems up.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:59.380
And tech sprawl, right?

00:25:59.380 --> 00:26:03.400
All of the extra hardware
that people bought,

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:06.070
that was taking up space
in our data center.

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:10.180
It was taking up electricity
and cooling our data center.

00:26:10.180 --> 00:26:11.680
People hoarded hardware.

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:13.720
When they stopped
using the system,

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:16.220
do you think they told
IT they're done using it?

00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:16.750
No.

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:17.680
They kept it around.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:20.260
They kept it running, because
I might need to run that.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:22.900
I might need that
for something later.

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:26.350
We actually found that
throughout our cloud migration

00:26:26.350 --> 00:26:29.320
process, we decommissioned
to 20% of what

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:33.830
was in our data center, because
it wasn't being used anymore.

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:35.838
So that was a huge cost.

00:26:35.838 --> 00:26:36.880
We've been patching that.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:39.047
We've been doing firmware
upgrades on those systems.

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:43.180
People wouldn't
share, because they'd

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:46.830
be hoarding that to themselves.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:50.400
So some key objectives that we
found for our transformation.

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:53.700
We wanted to kickstart
agility and speed

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:56.670
we wanted to reduce our
hardware acquisition burdens.

00:26:56.670 --> 00:27:00.720
We wanted to empower
our application teams.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.960
A very quick example,
when we looked

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:06.870
at our caseload
for our server team

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:08.810
and the number of
cases that came in,

00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:10.860
60% of the cases
that they got were

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:14.757
from a development team saying,
can you restart my server?

00:27:14.757 --> 00:27:17.090
And do you think that got
done when they needed it done,

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:21.450
or do you think it waited three
to five days for that SLA?

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:24.660
Let IT drive business,
not infrastructure.

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:28.020
We want to be doing the right
thing for the right reasons.

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:31.540
Reducing unneeded
labor and cost,

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:33.300
and also deploy self-service.

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:36.300
This was key to us achieving
a lot of the efficiencies

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.070
that we were looking
for as we needed

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:40.440
to enable self-service
to our application teams.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:47.530
So, about a year into
our cloud migration,

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:52.380
we decided to take another
look at what we're doing.

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:56.040
We had originally picked
another cloud provider.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:28:00.270
At the time, it was the only
enterprise-grade cloud around.

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:02.700
Then that cloud entered
the health business.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.220
Surprise.

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:08.460
So it obviously started
prompting some questions

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:11.220
in our business's mind and
in our customer's mind.

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:15.000
Are we on the right
cloud platform for us?

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:19.680
So we started an RFP.

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:22.890
We wanted to determine really
which cloud provider was

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:25.110
the best cloud provider,
not just because of business

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:29.100
drivers, but for
technological drivers, too.

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:32.543
If that cloud provider really
was the best solution, well,

00:28:32.543 --> 00:28:33.960
we're going to
have to suck it up,

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:36.240
because we were going to
have to do business, right?

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:38.760
If you're going to
continue being competitive,

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:42.460
we needed to be
getting into the cloud,

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:46.610
but obviously, we wanted
to see for ourselves.

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:50.080
Ultimately, GCP was
the cloud for us.

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:53.420
The things that
drove this decision

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:57.770
was a centralized organization,
a top-level organization, where

00:28:57.770 --> 00:28:59.120
you can federate your accounts.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:01.220
Users have one
place to log into.

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:02.630
They don't have to switch roles.

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:04.220
They don't have
to switch regions.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:05.637
They don't have
to switch accounts

00:29:05.637 --> 00:29:10.790
to try to access their
services or systems.

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:13.610
Individual projects
for application teams

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.300
really provide the autonomy.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:17.210
We couldn't do
self-service before,

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:22.040
because you couldn't scale
to hundreds of accounts

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:23.730
in that other cloud provider.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.780
But you couldn't have
300 application teams

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:28.400
each have their own account.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:30.150
It was not scalable.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:31.850
So projects really
gave that autonomy

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:34.160
to our application teams.

00:29:34.160 --> 00:29:36.740
And then shared
VPC concept really

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:38.720
allowed us to give
that autonomy,

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:41.510
but still, put enterprise
guardrails in place so we

00:29:41.510 --> 00:29:42.920
could protect the enterprise.

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:44.300
We needed to protect egress.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:47.440
We needed to protect ingress.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:50.020
Ease of granting access.

00:29:50.020 --> 00:29:54.370
There were no difficult
roles to have to understand.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:56.200
It was much, much
easier to deliver

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:58.360
self-service to our
application teams.

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:01.750
So we had a clear
winner in all the areas

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:03.230
that we were looking for.

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:06.310
In addition to the
future technology,

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:09.010
Google is a highly innovative.

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:12.730
And new things are coming
out very, very frequently

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:15.520
in the data analytics
space, everywhere,

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:17.180
in machine learning.

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:20.830
So in Kubernetes, these were
all also important to us,

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:27.180
not just for today's needs but
for tomorrow's needs as well.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:30.470
So as you mentioned
building our agile migration

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:32.690
factor in how we
actually migrate stuff,

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:37.160
so we chose a two-week
sprint process, where,

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:41.270
within that first week, we would
invest heavily on discovery

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:45.890
work and really understanding
what that application did,

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:48.260
how it worked, what
it communicated with,

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:51.470
what load balancers were in
front of it, what DNS names,

00:30:51.470 --> 00:30:54.500
where someone may
have had a static IP

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:58.250
address that they hid in
their configuration somewhere.

00:30:58.250 --> 00:31:02.630
And then the second week, we'd
get into migration preparation.

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:05.360
We'd be building those
automated runbooks.

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:11.690
We'd be preparing scripts
for updating DNS and doing

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:13.790
our updating single
sign-on, preparing

00:31:13.790 --> 00:31:19.250
firewall rules, et cetera, with
the end of that cycle being

00:31:19.250 --> 00:31:23.750
the actual migration, and then
application smoke testing.

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:28.460
So this was very
successful for us.

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:30.030
Since we were going
in to experience,

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:34.190
it also gave us the opportunity
to really iterate and improve

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:35.300
upon our processes.

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:42.260
So keys to migration
success, start small.

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:46.100
We didn't start with our most
complex production applications

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:47.630
to migrate into the cloud.

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:50.940
We started with the dev
systems, the test systems.

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:53.060
The very early dev
test systems as we

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:55.352
did with friendly application
teams that we knew.

00:31:55.352 --> 00:31:56.810
You all know the
difference between

00:31:56.810 --> 00:31:59.000
the friendly application
teams and the non-friendly

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:02.210
application teams.

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:05.900
Declare small early victories,
get comfortable with the cloud.

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:06.480
It's new.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:10.400
It's a big change for everyone.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:13.490
Starting small really lets
you have that opportunity

00:32:13.490 --> 00:32:17.570
to understand what you're doing,
discover the road bumps you're

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:19.790
going to run into and
then iterate and improve

00:32:19.790 --> 00:32:21.620
on those processes.

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:25.140
Building a cloud
culture is important.

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:28.010
The most difficult thing
about cloud migration

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:29.970
isn't migrating systems.

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:32.300
The most difficult thing
about cloud migration

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:35.840
is changing the people,
changing the processes.

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:39.230
So we put a lot of focus
on that organizationally

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:41.090
to be able to improve upon that.

00:32:44.212 --> 00:32:46.670
What ways we kept
off the momentum?

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:50.670
We use this as an opportunity
to train people on new skills?

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.190
We gamified migrations.

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:54.560
We had prizes.

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:58.250
Which application team could
migrate the most systems

00:32:58.250 --> 00:32:59.360
within a month?

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:03.620
Which teams would be
first into production?

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:06.260
Those kind of things.

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:08.600
We gave individual
projects a code name.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:10.290
We had a lot of fun.

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:12.710
We had posters.

00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:14.540
In the first phase
of our project,

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:19.560
every sprint had a movie
poster theme to it.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:21.560
So we did a lot of
stuff to have fun.

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:24.485
We brought lots of food in,
because everyone loves food.

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:29.430
Back to cost savings.

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:31.770
I mentioned that is one
of our primary drivers.

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:33.585
How did we obtain cost savings?

00:33:33.585 --> 00:33:35.460
Now, when you talk to
people about the cloud,

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:39.330
the first impression is,
oh, that's expensive.

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:42.780
Our migration to the cloud,
we had lean migration teams

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:46.240
that were highly focused
on what they were doing.

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:49.200
We upload our existing IT staff.

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:52.560
It wasn't a cleanse out
the whole IT department

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:55.540
and bring in new people.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:34:00.780
It was bringing in people from
areas that would otherwise

00:34:00.780 --> 00:34:02.340
be impacted.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:05.100
When you're going to cloud,
your storage team goes away,

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:07.320
your networking
team reduces a lot,

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:09.960
your OS teams reduce,
your VMware team.

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:12.480
We brought a lot of those
infrastructure teams

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:15.900
into our migration program,
got them trained up.

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:17.699
And they were some
of the people that

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:22.440
were leading our actual
migration activities.

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:23.730
We right-sized VMs.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:26.670
We found a lot of stuff
in our data center,

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:32.310
where this database server, it
was a physical database server.

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:36.360
It had 128 cores
and 512 giga memory.

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:37.260
And guess what?

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:40.770
It was running at 1% or 2%
CPU, and the database only

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:43.420
had 8 gig of memory
that it was running.

00:34:43.420 --> 00:34:46.590
So there's a lot
of stuff oversized,

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:51.370
because they never knew what
they might need in the future.

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:54.820
Again, I mentioned that we
decommissioned about 20%

00:34:54.820 --> 00:34:55.389
of systems.

00:34:55.389 --> 00:34:57.520
We never had to
migrate THEM because

00:34:57.520 --> 00:34:59.500
in talking to the
application teams,

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:00.880
they weren't really using them.

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:04.770
And when they found out, we
were covering cloud costs year

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:08.710
one, but year two,
they'd have those costs.

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:11.920
It made them think twice about
whether they really needed

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:13.785
that system that they
were hoarding anymore,

00:35:13.785 --> 00:35:15.160
especially when
we're giving them

00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:18.250
the keys to be able to build
new systems as they needed them.

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:21.190
So 20% reduction
in our VM footprint

00:35:21.190 --> 00:35:24.047
that we never had to migrate.

00:35:24.047 --> 00:35:26.380
Something else, though, is
critical to our savings, that

00:35:26.380 --> 00:35:28.900
was really a culture change
for application teams,

00:35:28.900 --> 00:35:30.700
is only power on
your systems when

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:33.890
you need them most
application teams,

00:35:33.890 --> 00:35:36.220
especially a lot of our
legacy applications,

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:37.900
they don't do frequent releases.

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:41.830
They're not hammering away
at their lower environments

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:42.700
frequently.

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:46.480
So our lower environments
are powered off 70%-80%

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:52.000
of the time; nights, weekends
business days when they're not

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:52.840
using them.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:54.700
We put automated
processes in place

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:58.750
to be able to let application
teams tag their instance.

00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:01.480
So at the time they
choose, every day

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:03.272
that system powers
off and give them

00:36:03.272 --> 00:36:05.230
the choice of whether it
power on automatically

00:36:05.230 --> 00:36:07.360
the next day or not.

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:09.220
So definitely powering
off your instances

00:36:09.220 --> 00:36:12.890
when you don't need them as key
to saving money in the cloud.

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:15.880
I give the analogy
of always telling

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:18.890
my kids to turn off the lights
when they leave the room.

00:36:18.890 --> 00:36:20.390
Do the same thing
with your servers.

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:22.890
When you leave for the day and
you're not using it, power it

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:23.440
off.

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:24.820
If you're going to be
in meetings all day,

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:26.050
don't power that server on.

00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:31.150
Agility was also a driver.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.760
Our time to cloud
dramatically decreased.

00:36:33.760 --> 00:36:37.930
We moved thousands of
systems into the cloud

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:39.130
within a couple of years.

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:43.720
And that included taking about
three to four-month hiatus

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:45.730
to change our cloud provider.

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:48.200
A lot of work went into that.

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:49.750
Just in time infrastructure.

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:52.390
Application teams have the
ability to spin up instances

00:36:52.390 --> 00:36:54.880
when they need them, spin
them down, delete them

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:56.540
if they don't need them.

00:36:56.540 --> 00:37:00.130
Again, they have self-service,
no more cumbersome procurement

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:02.890
process.

00:37:02.890 --> 00:37:05.800
Application teams
have a cloud budget,

00:37:05.800 --> 00:37:09.170
and they choose how to
spend that cloud budget

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:11.950
if they are going to
exceed that budget

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:15.030
and it just happens to be in
the same bucket as their labor

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:15.530
bucket.

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:19.510
So they really have
the ability to switch

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:22.390
a lot of levers for
departments to be

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:25.390
able to make their budgets.

00:37:25.390 --> 00:37:28.600
And the application
teams feel empowered.

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:31.490
empowered.

00:37:31.490 --> 00:37:34.370
So really feel empowered to be
in control of their destiny.

00:37:34.370 --> 00:37:36.460
They can do what they need done.

00:37:36.460 --> 00:37:38.680
They can achieve
their business goals

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:42.180
without relying on
infrastructure teams.

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:43.350
And people were convinced.

00:37:43.350 --> 00:37:45.017
People who were worried
about their role

00:37:45.017 --> 00:37:46.980
are excited about
the new technology

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:50.282
and learning new skills while
still at Cardinal Health.

00:37:50.282 --> 00:37:51.990
App teams who are
worried about the cloud

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:55.350
love their newfound
empowerment and agility.

00:37:55.350 --> 00:37:58.890
And compliance teams
worried about regulations

00:37:58.890 --> 00:38:03.360
now realize that
HIPAA, FDA, GDPR; these

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:07.390
are all easier to achieve in
the cloud, not more difficult.

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:10.650
There's so much more technology
available to you in the cloud

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:16.140
than you could ever build
in your own data center.

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:17.720
And you will hit speed bumps.

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:23.420
Don't expect this to be all
roses and cherry on top.

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:25.730
Everything's not easy.

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:29.790
People are going to have
fear, uncertainty, and doubt.

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:32.090
You need to remove that
fear, uncertainty, and doubt

00:38:32.090 --> 00:38:34.340
by sharing information,
being open and honest

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:36.260
with your people.

00:38:36.260 --> 00:38:38.270
We establish a Cloud
Center of Expertise

00:38:38.270 --> 00:38:41.600
to really uplift the
entire organization.

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:44.330
We held training events
we partnered with Google

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:48.710
to bring in experts from across
the field, whether it was

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:52.340
Kubernetes, or infrastructure,
or machine learning,

00:38:52.340 --> 00:38:55.370
or data and analytics.

00:38:55.370 --> 00:39:00.620
We really brought our teams up
to speed on current technology.

00:39:00.620 --> 00:39:05.190
And really you need to energize
the base of your teams.

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:11.930
From a process perspective,
industry validation,

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:14.020
compliance updating SOPs.

00:39:17.540 --> 00:39:20.450
There is a lot of process
things that come up

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:22.340
that you need to adapt to.

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:26.780
And being flexible and
adaptive are important aspects

00:39:26.780 --> 00:39:28.340
of moving to the cloud.

00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:29.570
It's not going to be--

00:39:29.570 --> 00:39:33.230
We joke that-- when
we find something

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:35.328
that works and find a
standard-- that, hey,

00:39:35.328 --> 00:39:36.620
that's our standard this month.

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:39.260
But we don't know what's going
to come next month, because we

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:42.890
don't know what challenges
are going to be facing us.

00:39:42.890 --> 00:39:47.510
From a technology perspective,
shared platforms are difficult.

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:50.540
Shared databases, and I know
you shouldn't share databases,

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:54.140
but our application
teams did it.

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:58.040
Web servers that you may have
15 different applications on.

00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:00.260
Those are a little
bit difficult to move.

00:40:00.260 --> 00:40:01.850
They take a little
bit more time.

00:40:01.850 --> 00:40:02.870
Firewall rules.

00:40:02.870 --> 00:40:05.120
For north-south
communication, you

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:07.250
need to understand
your firewall rules.

00:40:07.250 --> 00:40:13.130
Ingress and egress proxies, for
us, it was legacy SiteMinder.

00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:18.147
And a lot of that
configuration wasn't

00:40:18.147 --> 00:40:20.480
at the top of everyone's mind,
because that's been there

00:40:20.480 --> 00:40:23.330
and sitting for a while.

00:40:23.330 --> 00:40:27.800
Load balancers can be difficult.
And network-attached storage,

00:40:27.800 --> 00:40:28.820
don't forget about that.

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:30.740
You move your
machines, guess what?

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:34.470
You need to move their network
attached storage with it.

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:38.180
So as an SMB, don't forget
about those, because we

00:40:38.180 --> 00:40:39.410
had some surprises there.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:46.080
Lessons learned, we iterated.

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:49.740
We were continually improving
our process after every sprint,

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:52.590
making sure that the roadblocks
that we ran into that we

00:40:52.590 --> 00:40:54.480
don't run into them again.

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:55.720
In parallel discovery.

00:40:55.720 --> 00:40:59.220
Don't just try to do all
your discovery upfront

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:00.390
and then move to migration.

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:02.760
You won't gain the momentum.

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:10.980
You also want to make
sure that you're not

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:13.230
overanalyzing things
and not progressing,

00:41:13.230 --> 00:41:16.170
because you're trying to
do too much discovery.

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:19.320
Although I will say
too much discovery

00:41:19.320 --> 00:41:23.070
is a hard thing to achieve,
but don't spend too much time

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:25.230
overanalyzing things.

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:28.142
Automate everything as
he talked about runbooks.

00:41:28.142 --> 00:41:29.850
We've gotten to the
point towards the end

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:34.740
of our migration here that when
it comes to migration time,

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:38.050
it's literally someone
pushes a button,

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:42.940
and the migrations
occur, DNS is updated,

00:41:42.940 --> 00:41:46.740
the DNS configuration on
the boxes are updated,

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:50.430
software is installed,
software is uninstalled.

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:53.670
And there's very little
human interaction needed

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:56.610
with migrations, because
as we ran into things,

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:59.175
no one likes to do the same
thing more than once or twice.

00:41:59.175 --> 00:42:01.050
And if you're migrating
thousands of systems,

00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:03.810
you don't want to do the same
thing thousands of times.

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:05.233
ISSY BEN-SHAUL: For sure.

00:42:05.233 --> 00:42:06.900
LOUIS VERZI: Make a
migration checklist,

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:10.290
so you don't forget things and
batch your migration waves.

00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:13.110
As he talked about 50
to 100, that number

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:16.290
really worked well for us.

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:19.620
Most of our migration waves
were between 50 and 100 systems.

00:42:19.620 --> 00:42:21.480
And that was very
manageable for us.

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:24.040
It allowed us to get
our scale and our speed,

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:28.860
but without having too much
that we were overwhelmed.

00:42:28.860 --> 00:42:32.740
A final note, keep it
fun and have pizza.

00:42:32.740 --> 00:42:35.710
Everyone loves pizza.

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:39.940
Amazon coined the phrase
about the two pizza team.

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:41.260
That didn't work for us.

00:42:41.260 --> 00:42:45.940
I like two pizzas myself,
so I wouldn't get very far

00:42:45.940 --> 00:42:46.600
by myself.

00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:50.210
But we really had a lot of fun.

00:42:50.210 --> 00:42:54.530
We really worked hard, and
we ate a lot of food too.

00:42:54.530 --> 00:42:57.870
So, with that, I will
hand it over to Issy

00:42:57.870 --> 00:42:59.812
to share some roadmap items.

00:42:59.812 --> 00:43:01.020
ISSY BEN-SHAUL: Thanks, mate.

00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:02.332
Good job.

00:43:02.332 --> 00:43:04.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:05.380 --> 00:43:09.030
I thank Louis and his team for
being a great product for us.

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:11.550
When they moved to the first
cloud, we were still a startup.

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:13.590
And we help them move
to the first cloud,

00:43:13.590 --> 00:43:17.100
and then we did the
second move with them too.

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:20.190
The good thing is
that the service

00:43:20.190 --> 00:43:23.110
is free for people who want
to move to GCP, the migration

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:23.610
service.

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:28.560
Everything is free, as long as
you move to the right cloud.

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:32.230
I would have said just
one minute about what

00:43:32.230 --> 00:43:34.320
was announced today.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:38.190
And again, by the way, Cardinal
is one of our good design

00:43:38.190 --> 00:43:39.760
partners on the next step.

00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:43.800
So while you've seen now
the lift and shift is

00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:47.340
a very viable possibility
for large scale migrations,

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:50.100
we also see more and more
customers asking us, hey,

00:43:50.100 --> 00:43:52.290
but once we move to
the cloud, can we

00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:55.170
can get some help in
modernizing workloads,

00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:58.590
making them more containerized?

00:43:58.590 --> 00:44:00.420
Can we get some
operational efficiencies

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:03.450
of the cloud, developer
productivity, et cetera?

00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:07.140
And many of them
have tried or two--

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:09.070
And again, we're not
talking cloud-native

00:44:09.070 --> 00:44:11.790
but rather about existing
legacy applications.

00:44:11.790 --> 00:44:13.420
What can be done there?

00:44:13.420 --> 00:44:16.120
The commercial of the shelf
tools, et cetera, software.

00:44:16.120 --> 00:44:19.720
And when they start to get
through this process manually,

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:23.460
they find out that this is very
hard and challenging process.

00:44:23.460 --> 00:44:26.250
And that let us think
about what can we

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:29.580
do to help those customers
not only migrate to the Cloud

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:31.500
but also modernize.

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:33.390
And I don't have time
to elaborate that.

00:44:33.390 --> 00:44:35.800
There are actually multiple
sessions on that are given,

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:38.710
but just give you a glimpse
of what we've basically done.

00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:42.090
And that was the essence of
the announcement that was made

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:45.450
in the keynote about
Anthos-Migrate, which is--

00:44:45.450 --> 00:44:48.240
We came up with
this solution that

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:52.530
allows you essentially rely
on the infrastructure layer

00:44:52.530 --> 00:44:55.750
that we talk today for
transport and streaming.

00:44:55.750 --> 00:44:59.970
But, in addition to that, we're
able to take essentially VMs,

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:03.690
and essentially strip all the
system elements that we take,

00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:06.450
just the user mode
applications workload,

00:45:06.450 --> 00:45:09.780
and essentially convert
them into Kubernetes,

00:45:09.780 --> 00:45:13.478
detach the network and storage
that they've been working with

00:45:13.478 --> 00:45:15.270
and the operating system
kernel, et cetera,

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:18.680
and reconnect them into
Kubernetes, or particularly,

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:22.830
in GKE networking,
storage, et cetera.

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:27.840
And what happens is that,
out of this process,

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:30.420
using streaming but
also the transformation,

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:34.470
we're now getting really
in a zero-touch matter--

00:45:34.470 --> 00:45:36.390
VMs that we're running
previously on-prem

00:45:36.390 --> 00:45:41.100
running as actual Kubernetes
or actually GKE containers

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:43.810
and getting some of that
benefits out of the door

00:45:43.810 --> 00:45:44.310
right away.

00:45:44.310 --> 00:45:46.727
For instance, you don't have
to deal with operating system

00:45:46.727 --> 00:45:49.630
patches or security.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:52.800
You don't have to deal
with all the things

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:55.328
that come with a VM-based.

00:45:55.328 --> 00:45:57.120
Like for instance, you
can get the benefits

00:45:57.120 --> 00:46:00.000
of networking security.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.060
You can gather all the benefits
of visibility, monitoring,

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:03.777
logging.

00:46:03.777 --> 00:46:05.610
All this comes out of
the box without having

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:07.500
to deal with anything.

00:46:07.500 --> 00:46:11.760
And that has been really a jump.

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:16.530
Now, once we do that, we
can also add what's called

00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:19.590
[INAUDIBLE] and extend the
capability or augment it

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:20.130
by virtual--

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:21.797
For instance, putting
these two sidecar,

00:46:21.797 --> 00:46:25.110
which gives you a service
mesh capability, visibility,

00:46:25.110 --> 00:46:27.060
network security, all this.

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:30.960
And all this is done without
making any single line

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:34.120
of change in the code.

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:36.610
Now, this is our first only--

00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:38.520
we'll call phase
one, and it gives you

00:46:38.520 --> 00:46:41.790
the operational benefits of GKE.

00:46:41.790 --> 00:46:45.850
And what we are working on
and will release soon after

00:46:45.850 --> 00:46:49.050
is the ability also to
leverage the productivity

00:46:49.050 --> 00:46:50.980
through CI/CD pipelines.

00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:53.640
And that will be done by
actually not only migrating

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:56.560
the VMs but also
breaking them down,

00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:59.263
taking the docker images,
containers, elements out.

00:46:59.263 --> 00:47:00.930
They're artifacts and
being able for you

00:47:00.930 --> 00:47:03.780
to use image-based
management in the right way.

00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:07.530
So this is coming later
and leveraging the tools

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:11.370
that we have today to
introspect in Kubernetes.

00:47:11.370 --> 00:47:13.620
The dependencies and
use that in order

00:47:13.620 --> 00:47:15.712
to actually further
break down the monolith.

00:47:15.712 --> 00:47:17.670
This is something that
will come down the road,

00:47:17.670 --> 00:47:20.340
but we don't have
much time for that.

00:47:20.340 --> 00:47:22.710
I just wanted to give
you a glimpse of that.

00:47:22.710 --> 00:47:26.130
We actually are working with
a number of design partners

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:30.040
to further elaborate and
develop the solution.

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:31.500
One of them is Cardinal.

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:33.270
And the interesting
thing to keep in mind

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:36.210
is that our approach
to Anthos-Migrate

00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:39.270
is such that we want to meet
the customer where they are.

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:43.950
What I mean by that is that
you can actually transform VMs

00:47:43.950 --> 00:47:46.367
to containers, whether
you're on-premises,

00:47:46.367 --> 00:47:48.450
whether you're in another
cloud, or whether you're

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:51.870
already in GCP but have
not migrate yet to GKE.

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:54.090
So you can migrate first
and modernize later.

00:47:54.090 --> 00:47:57.208
For instance, Cardinal Health
has done thousands of VMs,

00:47:57.208 --> 00:47:59.250
but they are looking now
and further modernizing.

00:47:59.250 --> 00:48:01.800
And they can do that
with Anthos-Migrate

00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:04.410
from GCP into GKE.

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:09.870
We also will support the
ability to move to GKE On-Prem,

00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:13.920
and then you can, from there on,
move from GKE On-Prem to GKE.

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:16.057
And, finally, we also
support workloads

00:48:16.057 --> 00:48:17.640
that are running in
any other clouds--

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:20.670
I mean AWS-- and they
can move either to GCP

00:48:20.670 --> 00:48:23.670
or directly into GKE.

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:25.560
So all options are
ordered to give you

00:48:25.560 --> 00:48:27.630
the flexibility
you need to decide

00:48:27.630 --> 00:48:29.970
whether you want
to modernize first,

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:32.280
migrate later, or vice versa.

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:34.980
Are we getting
really out of time?

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:36.760
We have one minute
for questions.

00:48:36.760 --> 00:48:38.990
So thank you very much
for listening to us.

00:48:38.990 --> 00:48:39.922
OK.

00:48:39.922 --> 00:48:41.880
We're going to be here
a few minutes if someone

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:44.910
wants to come and have
some questions offline.

00:48:44.910 --> 00:48:48.210
But thank you very much
for attending today.

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:52.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

